{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>But indeed your blood, etc.</b> A warning against choking oneself. And they said in Bereshit Rabbah: ״יכול אפי' כחנניה מישאל ועזריה״ — ת\"ל ״אך״. That is: one might think even like those who delivered themselves to sanctify the Name — that one would be permitted to injure oneself if one fears one cannot withstand the trial; ת\"ל ״אך״ — only at a time of religious persecution may one deliver oneself to death and kill oneself. And so with Saul son of Kish, who said to his attendant: ״Draw your sword and run me through with it, etc.״ — ת\"ל ״אך״, meaning that if a person fears they may inflict upon him severe tortures that he cannot endure and withstand the trial, he may kill himself. And from here they bring proof for those who slaughter the child at the time of a decree. And some forbid, and they explain thus: ״יכול כחנניה וחבריו״ who had already been delivered to death — ת\"ל ״אך״ — but one may not kill oneself. ״יכול כשאול״ who delivered himself to death — ת\"ל ״אך״ — meaning one may not injure oneself at all; and Saul did so without the Sages' permission. This is from Maharam bar Abraham, nicknamed Ochman. And there was an incident involving a certain rabbi who slaughtered many children at the time of a religious persecution, for he feared they would make them transgress their faith. And there was another rabbi with him who was extremely angry with him and called him a murderer, but he paid no heed. And that rabbi said: if his words are correct, may that rabbi be killed an unusual death. And so it was: gentiles seized him and they flayed his skin and placed brimstone between the skin and the flesh, and afterward the decree was annulled. And had he not slaughtered those children, they would have survived."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:05:20.886822277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}